WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.880
To understand how data science relates to&nbsp;
epidemiology, it helps to go back and look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:19.760
John Snow, the first epidemiologist. John Snow was&nbsp;
a physician in England and he lived in the 1800s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.280
And he was known for being the leader in medical&nbsp;
hygiene and also a leading advocate for the use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.720
of anesthesia which apparently up to that&nbsp;
point was not very common. But, what John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.240
Snow is best known for is for having responded&nbsp;
to an outbreak of cholera in London in 1854.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.840
And what he did was relatively unusual.
He decided to chart the cases of cholera on a map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.520
He put a little mark where&nbsp;
somebody has developed cholera.&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.960
If we zoom in on the middle part, you can&nbsp;
see that right there in the center is Broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.000
Street and there is a lot of cases next to&nbsp;
one another on the south side of the street.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.280
He was able to go to that location&nbsp;
and determine that there was a water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.960
pump there and that people were getting&nbsp;
contaminated water from the water pump.&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.320
He took the very practical step of simply&nbsp;
removing the pump handle from the water pump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.880
so people couldn’t get water out of it anymore&nbsp;
and the cholera cases dropped off dramatically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.640
This is seen as the first step of modern&nbsp;
epidemiology or studying the origins and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.880
patterns of diseases. Now we can do&nbsp;
something a little more advanced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.160
that as opposed to simply getting out&nbsp;
some ink and putting marks on a map.&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.200
We can start looking at digital information for&nbsp;
patterns of disease. For example, Google was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.600
able to track the spread of flu by looking at&nbsp;
search trends for symptoms related to the flu.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.600
Another project by Wikipedia did the same thing&nbsp;
except for more diseases and more countries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.200
Similarly, a third group of researchers could&nbsp;
predict a small but important percentage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:01.520
cases of pancreatic cancer based on Bing searches.&nbsp;
Interestingly, all of those use people’s natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.920
interactions with technology and the internet&nbsp;
to try to see what’s going on in their lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.000
But data science can do more&nbsp;
than that with epidemiology.&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.840
One of the big tests of epidemiology is estimating&nbsp;
the prevalence of a disease. One research project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.760
has estimated the levels of dengue in Brazil from&nbsp;
Twitter posts. What was interesting about that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.640
they were able to do it both at a very local city&nbsp;
level and at a country level, and they were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.840
to do it both in real time and prospectively.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.440
Other researchers developed an app for&nbsp;
the smartphone called Flu Near You.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.440
It asks people for flu symptoms, they report into&nbsp;
it directly and so that’s going to the source.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.840
Another very common source of data is the 70,000&nbsp;
people who use the ProMED-mail mailing list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.560
And this is a news aggregator,&nbsp;
it brings together news stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:59.280
it brings together public health announcements,&nbsp;
clinical observations and that is considered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.600
very high-resolution and a highly accurate&nbsp;
source of data that allows people to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.680
outbreaks in one place or another because&nbsp;
people are specifically talking about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.000
in a professional environment.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.800
And then also another project has been looking&nbsp;
at the high-resolution or very high information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.240
and highly detailed data that comes from medical&nbsp;
claims, de-identified medical claims in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.840
as a way of tracking diseases by what people are&nbsp;
actually going to their doctor to go see. All of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.480
these allow you to estimate the prevalence of a&nbsp;
disease either by using naturally occurring data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.520
through Twitter posts or getting more specific&nbsp;
by asking the people or by asking professionals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.760
or even looking at the specific reports of when&nbsp;
people visit their healthcare professionals.&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.840
Data science is also able to help predict&nbsp;
outbreaks of diseases which allow you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:52.080
turn to plan an intervention. One interesting&nbsp;
project has been looking at GPS data from phones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.000
and that has permitted researchers to track&nbsp;
the geographical movement of people who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.360
been diagnosed with Ebola. These are people who&nbsp;
they know have the disease and they were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.120
see where they are moving and that allows&nbsp;
them to design interventions to hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.040
contain the risk of the disease spreading&nbsp;
either by encouraging the people not to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.840
or by preparing the new locations to deal&nbsp;
with a person who is carrying the disease.&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:19.680
Next, other researchers have used remote sensing&nbsp;
and satellite images to gather data about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.160
physical environment, the animal movement and&nbsp;
the human interactions of groups of people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.640
estimate how many people live in places that&nbsp;
are known to have great risk for the Zika virus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.480
This in turn allows you not to just see how common&nbsp;
it is and the patterns of movement, but also to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.640
see what sorts of movements and interactions&nbsp;
create the greatest risk and also what can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.560
be done to help reduce things proactively.
Finally, one of these straight ahead data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.680
science applications is that researchers have&nbsp;
been able to take historical data about epidemics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.920
and the spread and combine it with a diverse&nbsp;
range of data to create massive simulations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.680
These are very computationally intensive,&nbsp;
to generate these simulations of outbreaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.200
that allows them to estimate the&nbsp;
probability of various scenarios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.160
and to design interventions that match each of&nbsp;
those scenarios and their calculated probability.&nbsp;

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:15.120
Taken together, data science allow you to go from&nbsp;
the very simple but extraordinarily effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.400
of putting a dot on a piece of&nbsp;
paper to find where disease was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.560
to looking at the natural traces of Twitter,&nbsp;
to asking people specifically about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:27.280
information, to even creating really advanced&nbsp;
simulations, and all of them allow you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:32.720
estimate both the probability of disease, to find&nbsp;
the sources, to see how it spreads through time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.840
and hopefully to create interventions to&nbsp;
reduce the spread and the risk of disease.

